WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.283 --> 00:00:02.950
(ominous music)

00:00:04.663 --> 00:00:07.499
(waves rush)
(ominous music)

00:00:07.499 --> 00:00:09.290
(metal rattles)

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:11.400
- Every waterway in the United States

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.520
is supposed to be safe enough to swim in

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.577
every single day; but,
of course, they're not.

00:00:16.577 --> 00:00:19.327
(dramatic music)

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.900
I am getting into these
contaminated waterways.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.140
Someone's gotta risk something.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:29.210
If people see that image,
they start to ask themselves,

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:31.130
why is that guy in there?

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:34.155
That starts a discussion
that can lead to a clean up.

00:00:34.155 --> 00:00:36.905
(dramatic music)

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:46.350
(bright music)

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:49.110
My name's Christopher Swain, and I've swum

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:54.110
over 3,000 miles in over 25
different contaminated waterways

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:58.720
in North America. I wanna
advocate for these waterways,

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:02.200
and I can energize clean-ups.
(bright music)

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.040
I learned to swim as a kid.

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.776
I was the kid who didn't
wanna get out of the water.

00:01:06.776 --> 00:01:08.390
(bright music)

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:11.450
As I got older, and I think
this is true for many of us,

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:14.610
I started to become aware
that waters are contaminated

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:16.830
with everything from arsenic to zinc,

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:19.970
everything from human
waste to nuclear waste.

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:23.490
We saw our waterways as dumpsters.

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:27.380
We saw them as sewers; legally speaking,

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:31.550
since 1972 when the Federal
Clean Water Act passed,

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:35.950
all navigable U.S. waterways
have to be safe for swimming

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:39.673
and fishing and drinking; if
they're a fresh water creek.

00:01:39.673 --> 00:01:42.280
(bright music)

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:45.280
Dozens and dozens of
those waterways were in

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.620
and around New York City.

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:50.710
Newtown Creek is the worst of those.

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:54.460
It is contaminated with lots
of things: heavy metals,

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:56.760
toxic chemicals, raw sewage,

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:59.290
and one of the largest ongoing oil spills

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:01.470
in the history of the United States.

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:06.200
So, in some way, we stole
this waterway from ourselves.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.885
What I'm saying is let's turn that around.

00:02:08.885 --> 00:02:11.468
(bright music)

00:02:13.491 --> 00:02:15.610
(upbeat music)

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:19.860
When I decide to take on a waterway,

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:20.693
this is my

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:24.800
puncture-resistant dry suit.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:27.380
I take all these measures on all my swims

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:29.940
to protect my body and
to protect my health.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:33.170
I wear a cap; I wear
goggles; I wear ear plugs;

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:35.820
I never put my head under, even so,

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:37.680
I've gotten sick lots of times.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.480
Rashes, skin infections, ear infections,

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:44.503
respiratory infections.
(upbeat music)

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:48.087
It's a hazard that goes
with the territory.

00:02:48.087 --> 00:02:51.650
(upbeat music)
The reason nobody

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:55.120
has swum the length of
Newtown Creek in history

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:58.320
is that at least in the last 100 years,

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.420
it's been far too disgusting.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:02.429
No one in their right
mind would go in there.

00:03:02.429 --> 00:03:05.012
(upbeat music)

00:03:06.251 --> 00:03:09.001
(dramatic music)

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:13.110
It's like swimming through a dirty diaper

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:18.110
that's been garnished with
oil, gasoline, and trash.

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:21.870
Every part of your skin wants to crawl.

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:24.540
So why do I push on and do it?

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:28.660
I'm doing this out of love,
like I really do love the water,

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:31.200
and I don't have a lot to work with.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:34.630
I'm not a wealthy person;
I'm not a scientist.

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:39.140
What I've got is my body
and my life to offer.

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:42.450
I'm gonna keep going back in my dry suit

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:45.730
and my hazmat gear until
these waterways are clean;

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:48.020
and when they're clean, I'm gonna get

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:50.430
into these clean waterways
in my bathing suit

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:52.670
and enjoy them with my fellow citizens.

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:55.253
(upbeat music)

00:03:58.634 --> 00:04:01.217
(bright music)

